the drives failed.  Lucky for me, noone at my company has *ever* had
to replace one before, so all this is completely new to me.  I believe
I have the steps I need to do but would like some verification,
suggestion:

:hwmgr -show scsi

        SCSI                DEVICE    DEVICE  DRIVER NUM  DEVICE FIRST
 HWID:  DEVICEID HOSTNAME   TYPE      SUBTYPE OWNER  PATH FILE   VALID
PATH
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
   65:  0        es40g      disk      none    0      1    dsk0  <---
failed drive
   66:  1        es40g      disk      none    2      1    dsk1
[0/1/0]
   67:  2        es40g      cdrom     none    0      1    cdrom0
[1/0/0]

HWID 65 is the failed drive.

:volprint -Adt
Disk group: rootdg

DM NAME         DEVICE       TYPE     PRIVLEN  PUBLEN   STATE

dm dsk0h        -            -        -        -        NODEVICE
dm dsk1d-AdvFS  dsk1d        nopriv   0        16672768 -
dm dsk1h        dsk1h        simple   4096     13848    -
dm root01       dsk0a        nopriv   0        2097152  FAILING
dm root02       dsk1a        nopriv   0        2097152  -
dm swap01       dsk0b        nopriv   0        16777216 -
dm swap02       dsk1b        nopriv   0        16777216 -
dm usr01        dsk0d        nopriv   0        16672768 FAILING

**remove the disk from lsm**
:voldg -k rmdisk root01
:voldg -k rmdisk usr01
:voldg -k rmdisk swap01
:voldg -k rmdisk dsk0h
:voldisk rm dsk0

**remove the disk from hwmgr**
:hwmgr del scsi -did 0
:hwmgr show scsi  (make sure its gone)

Physically swap the drives

**scan for the new drive**
:hwmgr scan scsi
:hwmgr show scsi  (until the drive shows up)

:hwmgr redirect scsi -src 0 -dest whatever_the_new_DID_is

**Add the new disk to lsm**
: disklabel -rwn dsk0
:voldisksetup -i dsk0
:voldg -g rootdg -k adddisk root01=dsk0
:voldg -g rootdg -k adddisk usr01=dsk0
:voldg -g rootdg -k adddisk swap01=dsk0
:voldg -g rootdg -k adddisk dsk0h=dsk0

**recover the volume**
:volrecover -sb rootdg

Or would it be
:volrecover -sb rootvol
:volrecover -sb usrvol
:volrecover -sb swapvol

since volinfo shows:
usrvol         fsgen    Started
rootvol        root     Started
swapvol        swap     Started


Thanks for any info, pointers, etc.

David



